JG Miller wrote:

 > The relevance of "ownership" here never fails to boggle me.

Which parts of this are too hard for you to
understand?

          NetHack, Copyright 1985-2003
          By Stichting Mathematisch Centrum and M. Stephenson.

That is a perfectly valid claim under the Berne
Copyright Convention, and the "relevance" is that
copyright is a right under law, the violation of
which carries heavy financial penalties.

 > The code and the name were inherited from still
 > other people in the past,

So? Inheritance is a perfectly valid way to transfer
legal ownership.

 > and there's no profit involved.

Which has spit to do with ownership. If I own a
house, there's not any need for it to run a profit
for me to own it.

 > The very name net-hack underlines its cooperative
 > heritage.

Yep, some couple of _hundred_ people invested real
effort into creating it, most of whom never met face
to face. But they _cotributed effort_, not whining.

 > It's a project perfectly suited for shared, active
 > development with its enthusiastic fan base, not
 > keeping away from others in the name of property
 > rights.

The problem isn't "keeping away in the name of
property rights", it's "keeping in the hands of
active, knowledgable *contributors*".

Notice that you _already_ have access to the source
code, and were you doing anything useful with that
access, you might well _be_ a member of the dev
team.

It isn't that hard to learn programming in general.

     A single programming language can be learned to
     the point of usability by an experienced
     programmer IN A SINGLE DAY. that's how long it
     took me to learn the programming language for
     dBase IV; I opened the manual on boarding a
     plane from California via Hawaii to Guam, got
     off the plane in Guam, went to the Navy base
     there and started solving problems they were
     having with their tropical cyclone forecasting
     automated assistant system using that coding
     language.

If you want to contribute to the NetHack community,
there are lots of things you can do, but most of
them require you to learn the language in which
NetHack is programmed _first_.

